  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> If you earn over <strong>€77,400/year</strong> (gross, 2025 threshold), you can choose between public (GKV) and private (PKV) health insurance in Germany. Most expats at this income level are significantly overpaying in GKV.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gkv-vs-pkv">GKV vs PKV — what's the difference?</h2>

  <p>Germany has two health insurance systems running in parallel. Almost everyone in Germany must have health insurance — it's the law. But at higher income levels, you get a choice.</p>

  <p><strong>GKV (Gesetzliche Krankenversicherung)</strong> — public health insurance. Contributions are calculated as a percentage of your gross salary (around 14.6% + supplemental fees, split with your employer). The more you earn, the more you pay — up to a cap.</p>

  <p><strong>PKV (Private Krankenversicherung)</strong> — private health insurance. Premiums are based on your individual health profile and the plan you choose, not your salary. This is why higher earners often pay significantly less in PKV than GKV.</p>

  <h2 id="who-qualifies">Who qualifies for PKV?</h2>

  <p>As a salaried employee, you can switch to PKV if your gross annual salary exceeds the <strong>Jahresarbeitsentgeltgrenze (JAEG)</strong>. In 2025, this threshold is <strong>€77,400/year</strong>.</p>

  <p>If you are self-employed or a freelancer (<em>Freiberufler / Gewerbetreibender</em>), you can opt into PKV regardless of income — and it is usually your best option, since GKV for self-employed people costs over <strong>€1,300/month</strong>.</p>

  <p>Nationality does not matter. You don't need to be German or have a German passport to get PKV.</p>

  <h2 id="costs">What does it actually cost?</h2>

  <p>This is where it gets interesting. Let's compare a typical expat profile earning €80,000/year:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Insurance type</th><th>Monthly cost</th><th>Annual cost</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>GKV (public)</td><td>~€620/month</td><td>~€7,440/year</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PKV (private, good plan)</td><td>~€300–380/month</td><td>~€3,600–4,560/year</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Annual saving</strong></td><td><strong>€240–320/month</strong></td><td><strong>€2,880–3,840/year</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>These are real numbers, not marketing estimates. The exact saving depends on your age, health history, and the plan you choose.</p>

  <h2 id="myths">Common myths about PKV, debunked</h2>

  <p><strong>Myth: "Once you're in PKV, you can never go back to GKV."</strong><br/>
  Partially true — but switching back IS possible under certain conditions (becoming self-employed, income dropping below the threshold, or certain life events). We explain exactly when and how.</p>

  <p><strong>Myth: "PKV gets unaffordable when you're older."</strong><br/>
  Contributions do rise with age — but German law requires PKV providers to build up <em>Alterungsrückstellungen</em> (age reserves) throughout your life, which slow this down significantly. The right plan choice at the start makes a huge difference.</p>

  <p><strong>Myth: "The insurance company can cancel my policy if I get sick."</strong><br/>
  False. PKV contracts in Germany are legally protected. You cannot be cancelled for getting sick, making claims, or developing a chronic condition.</p>

  <p><strong>Myth: "I've been here 10 years — I must have missed my chance."</strong><br/>
  Not necessarily. Depending on your current situation, switching may still be possible. The only way to know is to check — which takes about 15 minutes with the right advisor.</p>

  <h2 id="how-to-switch">How to switch from GKV to PKV</h2>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Check your eligibility</strong> — confirm your salary exceeds €77,400 gross/year</li>
    <li><strong>Get a comparison</strong> — compare plans from multiple PKV providers (not just one insurer)</li>
    <li><strong>Choose your plan</strong> — key things to check: deductible levels, dental coverage, hospital room type, vision coverage</li>
    <li><strong>Apply and get approved</strong> — PKV requires a health questionnaire; pre-existing conditions may affect premiums</li>
    <li><strong>Cancel your GKV</strong> — give 2 months' notice after receiving your PKV confirmation letter</li>
  </ol>

  <p>The whole process typically takes 2–4 weeks. The biggest mistake people make is comparing the wrong things or going directly to an insurer who only shows you their own products.</p>

